  "text": "A method is outlined for extracting a radioactive cesium isotope from concentrated nuclear waste using two types of solid inorganic ion‑exchange materials. First, a coarse‑grained ammonium‑based molybdenum exchanger captures most of the cesium when the waste solution passes through a column, after which a weak nitric acid wash removes virtually all other radio‑nuclides except trace amounts of zirconium and niobium isotopes. The cesium‑laden exchanger is then dissolved in a strong alkaline solution, the resulting precipitate is filtered out, and the filtrate is adjusted to a mildly acidic pH. This solution is passed through a second column containing a zirconium‑phosphate based exchanger, which separates cesium from the abundant sodium ions. Finally, the cesium is stripped from this second column with dilute hydrochloric acid, yielding a high‑purity cesium chloride product. To produce about ten megacuries per year, a daily 15‑liter column of the first exchanger and two or three reusable columns of the second are sufficient. The process requires relatively small equipment, minimal shielding, works with varied waste streams, adds little or no extra chemicals, and does not need prior waste neutralization."
